    <main>
        <h1>How an Operating System Works</h1>

        <figure class="page-graphic">
            <img src="/img/os-work.svg" alt="Diagram connecting process scheduling, memory, files, and devices to the operating system kernel">
            <figcaption>The kernel coordinates several core operating-system responsibilities.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>An operating system coordinates the computer. It gives programs the resources they need while helping the whole system stay responsive and organized.</p>

        <h2>Scheduling processes</h2>
        <p>The operating system decides which program gets processor time and when. It switches between tasks quickly, making it seem as though many programs run at once.</p>

        <h2>Managing memory</h2>
        <p>Each running program needs space for its data. The operating system tracks memory use and helps prevent programs from interfering with one another.</p>

        <h2>Organizing files</h2>
        <p>Directories and file systems organize stored information. The operating system helps create, find, and protect files.</p>

        <h2>Communicating with devices</h2>
        <p>Device drivers help the operating system communicate with hardware such as keyboards, screens, printers, and network adapters.</p>

        <h2>The kernel</h2>
        <p>The kernel is the central part of an operating system. It handles essential tasks such as managing hardware access and coordinating running programs.</p>
